# Editorial review — Movement 003, chapters 15–21

## Review status and coverage

This is an informed editorial review of all seven supplied chapters in declared order, with the supplied book map, state ledger, movement brief, edition brief, canon rules, universe bible, formula, measured metrics, and source-overlap summary. It is not a real-human read, not a manuscript-only cold read, and not an author self-review.

Coverage is complete for chapters 15–21 only. The book is incomplete, so book-level POV allocation, progression front-loading, and development-beat distribution remain provisional.

## Unscored reader response

Interest catches when the harmless fame tally produces `TA, assessment office: 0 of 7`, then strengthens as each failed method teaches Cael how to ask a better question. The empty-gallery test, the polished lamp, the council vote, and Seln sitting inside the feeling he has created in other people are excellent successive payoffs. The strongest emotional material is not the surveillance itself but the friends' different answers to it: Lira's protective anger, Karis's frightened arithmetic, Brom's plain strategic sense, and Cael's willingness to name the price he is choosing to pay.

Interest slips somewhat during the repeated explanation of the watcher model across chapters 15–18. The deductions are good, but several are stated, demonstrated, and then restated in metaphor. A fluent thirteen-year-old can follow the individual steps, yet the accumulated tallies, day numbers, maps, declaration theory, tax-rate analogy, and fatigue curve ask for sustained concentration before the bracket action arrives.

Cael matters most because his curiosity is inseparable from accountability: he keeps the failed maps, records the bruise, and writes the cost before he can pretend it was free. Lira is the strongest secondary attachment in this movement, with Seln close behind as the freshest complication. The next expected event is the system forcing these badly fitted people into more consequential measurements: Lira climbing the wrong bracket, Brom learning what his expensive correction costs, and Cael turning the watcher study into a decision. I would continue.

## Reader lenses

The two score columns are editorial lenses, not demographic research or a release gate. Confidence describes the judgment, not statistical certainty.

